our costs go up even
further, so special thanks

00:00:34.528 --> 00:00:38.128
to Bank of America, Google.org,
AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis

00:00:38.128 --> 00:00:41.558
and many of our supporters,
large and small.

00:00:41.558 --> 00:00:42.598
Thank you for that help.

00:00:42.598 --> 00:00:44.078
It's what allows us to keep going

00:00:44.078 --> 00:00:47.438
and serve tens of millions of
students around the planet,

00:00:47.438 --> 00:00:50.278
but we also do need more
help, especially for 2021.

00:00:50.278 --> 00:00:52.978
So if you're in a position to
do so, please think about it.

00:00:52.978 --> 00:00:55.468
And then my last announcement, reminder

00:00:55.468 --> 00:00:58.258
that this aversion of this live stream

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:02.528
is available in podcast form
Homeroom with Sal the podcast,

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:06.028
wherever you might find your podcasts.

00:01:06.028 --> 00:01:07.248
So with that,

00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:11.261
I'm super excited to introduce
our guest Marley Dias.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.408
She's an author.

00:01:13.408 --> 00:01:15.761
She's the founder of #1000BlackGirlBooks.

00:01:18.918 --> 00:01:21.238
Marley, thank you for joining us.

00:01:21.238 --> 00:01:22.538
- Thank you for having me.

00:01:23.508 --> 00:01:27.088
- I think you might be our
youngest guests so far,

00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:29.638
so you've already set a record (chuckles).

00:01:29.638 --> 00:01:34.328
- I wouldn't be surprised, I
hear youngest all the time.

00:01:34.328 --> 00:01:35.438
- Okay. (chuckles)

00:01:35.438 --> 00:01:37.518
You should enjoy it
because I used to hear that

00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:39.285
and I don't any longer.

00:01:39.285 --> 00:01:41.218
(chuckles)

00:01:41.218 --> 00:01:42.458
So, so, you know, there's,

00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:44.058
there's a bunch of
really interesting things

00:01:44.058 --> 00:01:47.158
that you've been working on and
maybe a good place to start.

00:01:47.158 --> 00:01:52.158
Explain what #1000BlackGirlBooks
is and why you started it.

00:01:53.808 --> 00:01:55.798
- So at campaign I've launched this called

00:01:55.798 --> 00:01:58.248
1000 Black Girl Books and it has the goal

00:01:58.248 --> 00:01:59.838
of collecting and donating books

00:01:59.838 --> 00:02:01.798
where black girls are the main characters

00:02:01.798 --> 00:02:03.495
to communities across the world.

00:02:03.495 --> 00:02:06.708
And this originated from
me being a lifelong reader,

00:02:06.708 --> 00:02:08.948
loving to read so much in fifth grade,

00:02:08.948 --> 00:02:11.318
but my teacher not having diverse books

00:02:11.318 --> 00:02:14.018
within our assigned
readings and reading lists.

00:02:14.018 --> 00:02:15.878
So I knew from my personal experience,

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:18.458
loving to read at home, that
there were so many stories

00:02:18.458 --> 00:02:21.018
that included the
experiences of black girls

00:02:21.018 --> 00:02:21.988
and people of color

00:02:21.988 --> 00:02:24.298
and people with different
experiences than mine.

00:02:24.298 --> 00:02:25.608
But when I went to school,

00:02:25.608 --> 00:02:29.248
I never had access to the
stories that I valued so much.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:30.958
So with 1000 Black Girl Books

00:02:30.958 --> 00:02:33.848
my original goal was to collect 1000 books

00:02:33.848 --> 00:02:36.248
where black ghosts like me
were the main character.

00:02:36.248 --> 00:02:37.928
And now over the past five years,

00:02:37.928 --> 00:02:40.248
I've collected 13,000 books

00:02:40.248 --> 00:02:41.918
where black girls are the main characters

00:02:41.918 --> 00:02:43.568
and been able to donate thousands

00:02:43.568 --> 00:02:45.808
to not only promote
diversity and inclusion,

00:02:45.808 --> 00:02:47.978
but to also show to other
kids that it is possible

00:02:47.978 --> 00:02:50.748
to care about something
super deeply at a young age,

00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:52.788
and to pursue it with the
help of your community

00:02:52.788 --> 00:02:54.238
and the help of your parents.

00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:56.248
- And I wanna double click on that,

00:02:56.248 --> 00:02:58.118
but I wanna remind everyone
watching, you know,

00:02:58.118 --> 00:03:00.278
whether you're watching
on Facebook or YouTube,

00:03:00.278 --> 00:03:02.818
please put questions in,
our team is looking at 'em.

00:03:02.818 --> 00:03:06.128
We will surface them to Marley
or myself if applicable,

00:03:06.128 --> 00:03:07.938
but I wanna double click
on what you just said,

00:03:07.938 --> 00:03:09.738
because I think there's
a lot of fifth graders,

00:03:09.738 --> 00:03:11.868
you know, 10 year olds,
which is when you started,

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:12.928
who will look at the world

00:03:12.928 --> 00:03:14.758
and say, I wish the world
was a little bit different.

00:03:14.758 --> 00:03:16.718
I wish I could change that aspect,

00:03:16.718 --> 00:03:18.788
but they're like, "I'm 10
years old, what can I do?"

00:03:18.788 --> 00:03:20.458
I mean, I know 30 year
olds who think that way,

00:03:20.458 --> 00:03:22.568
I'm 30 years old, what can I do?

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:24.478
much less if I'm 10 years old.

00:03:24.478 --> 00:03:26.458
What was it in your mind when you were 10

00:03:26.458 --> 00:03:28.218
and said, no, I think I
can make a difference.

00:03:28.218 --> 00:03:30.148
And how did you do that?

00:03:30.148 --> 00:03:31.518
How did you put one foot
in front of the other

00:03:31.518 --> 00:03:34.458
and start making progress
towards this initiative?

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:36.048
- Well, I think that's
a difficult question

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.578
because although I had created something,

00:03:38.578 --> 00:03:39.848
I wasn't always confident

00:03:39.848 --> 00:03:41.388
that I would be able to achieve it.

00:03:41.388 --> 00:03:42.888
So the reason I had a thousand is

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:45.188
because in my fifth grade
mind, not knowing, you know,

00:03:45.188 --> 00:03:46.978
like all of the numbers
that I can count to,

00:03:46.978 --> 00:03:48.448
I thought a thousand was

00:03:48.448 --> 00:03:50.788
the largest number I could think of.

00:03:50.788 --> 00:03:53.981
So I felt like that was
a reasonable goal for me,

00:03:55.078 --> 00:03:56.368
reasonable yet egregious.

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.418
So I think for a lot
of kids, they come, it,

00:03:58.418 --> 00:03:59.748
it really starts with research

00:03:59.748 --> 00:04:01.208
and understanding the problem.

00:04:01.208 --> 00:04:03.588
So at first I thought that only my school

00:04:03.588 --> 00:04:05.588
and only my school in
West Orange, New Jersey

00:04:05.588 --> 00:04:07.498
was the only school that wasn't diverse.

00:04:07.498 --> 00:04:09.088
But as soon as I complained to my mom,

00:04:09.088 --> 00:04:12.188
she was explaining to me that
her parents had this issue.

00:04:12.188 --> 00:04:14.438
My dad's great-grandparents had this issue

00:04:14.438 --> 00:04:17.148
and that it exists in a
larger scheme of things.

00:04:17.148 --> 00:04:20.268
So I think my first step for
kids before they decide to

00:04:20.268 --> 00:04:23.688
take action or step out
somewhere and try to protest

00:04:23.688 --> 00:04:26.468
and fight against something
or to fight for something,

00:04:26.468 --> 00:04:27.598
is to do more research

00:04:27.598 --> 00:04:30.078
and to understand the ways
in which this problem exists

00:04:30.078 --> 00:04:32.458
in your community and
then in the whole world,

00:04:32.458 --> 00:04:35.041
and then kind of a larger
grand scheme of things.

00:04:36.178 --> 00:04:37.011
- And how did that happen?

00:04:37.011 --> 00:04:38.618
So it sounds like, you
know, your first step

00:04:38.618 --> 00:04:39.868
you started talking to
your parents about it.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.598
You learned that this was a broader issue

00:04:41.598 --> 00:04:44.808
than just what you're facing
at your school or your area.

00:04:44.808 --> 00:04:47.598
I at age 10, I mean, how
did you say well, okay.

00:04:47.598 --> 00:04:50.078
What, how did you decide to take action?

00:04:50.078 --> 00:04:50.911
What was some of,

00:04:50.911 --> 00:04:52.387
what were some of those
first steps that you took?

00:04:52.387 --> 00:04:54.478
- One of the first steps was figuring out

00:04:54.478 --> 00:04:56.138
what books do I have in my home

00:04:56.138 --> 00:04:58.258
that I think every student should have,

00:04:58.258 --> 00:05:00.118
or what books do I really care about

00:05:00.118 --> 00:05:01.658
that I think public schools need.

00:05:01.658 --> 00:05:04.038
So it was "Brown Girl
Dreaming" by Jacqueline Woodson

00:05:04.038 --> 00:05:05.498
my favorite book at the time.

00:05:05.498 --> 00:05:07.948
And my mom had, and she's
still one of my favorites,

00:05:07.948 --> 00:05:09.138
but I'm not 10 anymore.

00:05:09.138 --> 00:05:11.788
So it's not necessarily
my favorite right now.

00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:13.298
I, my mom would make me take pictures,

00:05:13.298 --> 00:05:14.648
posing with the books.

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:17.408
And what we would do is
she had an office space

00:05:17.408 --> 00:05:20.158
and we would post the places
you could send the books.

00:05:20.158 --> 00:05:21.015
And I would say,

00:05:21.015 --> 00:05:23.108
my daughter, Marley is a lifelong reader.

00:05:23.108 --> 00:05:23.941
She really loves to read,

00:05:23.941 --> 00:05:25.698
but she cannot find books

00:05:25.698 --> 00:05:27.088
where black goes with the main character.

00:05:27.088 --> 00:05:28.618
So after I understood that,

00:05:28.618 --> 00:05:30.328
Oh, Marley faces this issue,

00:05:30.328 --> 00:05:32.988
but thousands of people
of color across the nation

00:05:32.988 --> 00:05:34.388
faced this issue as well,

00:05:34.388 --> 00:05:37.248
please send to here and
here, if you can donate.

00:05:37.248 --> 00:05:38.858
Now, people may think that I got

00:05:38.858 --> 00:05:41.518
an outpouring of love and
support in the first month,

00:05:41.518 --> 00:05:42.528
but I did not.

00:05:42.528 --> 00:05:44.358
I got some family, friends here,

00:05:44.358 --> 00:05:46.658
some random people on the internet here.

00:05:46.658 --> 00:05:48.878
And it wasn't necessarily
what I had expected

00:05:48.878 --> 00:05:51.928
that I would reach a thousand
and become this large success.

00:05:51.928 --> 00:05:53.688
And now I've learned to cheat that

00:05:53.688 --> 00:05:55.218
over a longer period of time.

00:05:55.218 --> 00:05:57.378
But I think for a lot of kids,

00:05:57.378 --> 00:05:59.228
it's about using the
power of social media,

00:05:59.228 --> 00:06:00.725
asking your parents to say,

00:06:00.725 --> 00:06:02.695
"Hey, can I talk to my
teachers and ask them

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:06.265
"if we can put a drop, a
drop box in our classroom?"

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:09.218
"Hey, can I talk to my
principal and my track coach

00:06:09.218 --> 00:06:10.418
and my soccer coach."

00:06:10.418 --> 00:06:12.648
So a lot of the times it's
really reaching out to people

00:06:12.648 --> 00:06:14.738
that already exist within your space.

00:06:14.738 --> 00:06:17.538
And I use social media because
I know it was a helpful tool

00:06:17.538 --> 00:06:20.198
for people that may live super far away,

00:06:20.198 --> 00:06:21.848
but it's also important
to reach out to people

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:23.538
that we know our posts,

00:06:23.538 --> 00:06:25.728
people that are our postman, our teachers,

00:06:25.728 --> 00:06:27.081
and our community members.

00:06:28.468 --> 00:06:30.578
- I think there's two layers

00:06:30.578 --> 00:06:32.088
at which your initiative
is really interesting.

00:06:32.088 --> 00:06:33.618
One is the direct layer
that you're talking.

00:06:33.618 --> 00:06:35.178
I mean, maybe actually three layers.

00:06:35.178 --> 00:06:37.208
There's the need to collect, you know,

00:06:37.208 --> 00:06:39.928
a thousand plus books for
maybe your local community,

00:06:39.928 --> 00:06:41.048
your local schools.

00:06:41.048 --> 00:06:42.678
I think there's a point in the signal

00:06:42.678 --> 00:06:44.068
that it sends to the broader world

00:06:44.068 --> 00:06:46.808
that like we should have more diversity

00:06:46.808 --> 00:06:48.508
in the books we read.

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:50.318
And I'd actually think
the other meta point

00:06:50.318 --> 00:06:52.738
is what we're talking about,
which is that, you know,

00:06:52.738 --> 00:06:55.208
someone between the ages of 10 and 15

00:06:55.208 --> 00:06:56.768
has a lot of agency in this world,

00:06:56.768 --> 00:06:58.208
maybe because of social media,

00:06:58.208 --> 00:06:59.608
maybe because of other things

00:06:59.608 --> 00:07:01.368
to actually get people thinking

00:07:01.368 --> 00:07:03.798
and get getting people to take action.

00:07:03.798 --> 00:07:06.528
When did you realize that this was more

00:07:06.528 --> 00:07:08.738
than just a little project
where you're, you know,

00:07:08.738 --> 00:07:09.918
saying, "Hey, let's get some books."

00:07:09.918 --> 00:07:11.368
It's kind of a book drive.

00:07:11.368 --> 00:07:12.201
When did you say,

00:07:12.201 --> 00:07:14.618
Hey, this is going to
really mean something,

00:07:14.618 --> 00:07:16.788
and I should put more energy into it.

00:07:16.788 --> 00:07:20.048
- So I started a campaign
in November of 2015,

00:07:20.048 --> 00:07:22.628
and that was me sitting
at the diner with my mom,

00:07:22.628 --> 00:07:25.308
complaining to her about how
much I thought fifth grade

00:07:25.308 --> 00:07:27.958
could have been better with better books.

00:07:27.958 --> 00:07:30.728
And then after months and months,

00:07:30.728 --> 00:07:32.138
and I, my goal was in February,

00:07:32.138 --> 00:07:34.898
I had collected only about 200 books

00:07:34.898 --> 00:07:37.358
within the span of three or so months.

00:07:37.358 --> 00:07:39.378
And I was so disappointed in myself,

00:07:39.378 --> 00:07:42.538
but towards the beginning of January

00:07:42.538 --> 00:07:44.668
and kind of early February as well,

00:07:44.668 --> 00:07:46.708
there were people in Philadelphia
that were reaching out

00:07:46.708 --> 00:07:49.868
for me to do radio interviews
and to talk about this.

00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:52.448
And when people started
mentioning that this was

00:07:52.448 --> 00:07:53.888
more of a systemic issue

00:07:53.888 --> 00:07:57.318
and that the idea of not having
diversity within classrooms

00:07:57.318 --> 00:08:00.588
existed for decades before my generation,

00:08:00.588 --> 00:08:02.098
I thought, was when I realized

00:08:02.098 --> 00:08:04.298
maybe this can connect to
more people than I imagined,

00:08:04.298 --> 00:08:06.278
because I really only
thought at the beginning

00:08:06.278 --> 00:08:08.258
that it would be teachers
that understood the problem

00:08:08.258 --> 00:08:10.418
that we're in classrooms and students,

00:08:10.418 --> 00:08:11.968
but then understanding that, you know,

00:08:11.968 --> 00:08:14.078
everyone in the United
States has to go to school

00:08:14.078 --> 00:08:14.998
at some point.

00:08:14.998 --> 00:08:16.258
And now we're kind of forcing people

00:08:16.258 --> 00:08:18.238
to think back on their experiences

00:08:18.238 --> 00:08:19.071
and to realize that

00:08:19.071 --> 00:08:20.988
there were so many inequities they faced,

00:08:20.988 --> 00:08:23.328
and now you can help a
young person like me,

00:08:23.328 --> 00:08:25.128
who is now experiencing these inequities,

00:08:25.128 --> 00:08:26.448
hopefully stop them

00:08:26.448 --> 00:08:28.898
and stop them for future
generations of students.

00:08:28.898 --> 00:08:30.748
So it was when I heard
my grandparents saying

00:08:30.748 --> 00:08:31.998
that they experienced this

00:08:31.998 --> 00:08:34.508
and hearing my parents say
that they experienced this

00:08:34.508 --> 00:08:36.058
really opened my eyes to the idea

00:08:36.058 --> 00:08:37.908
that I could connect with more people.

00:08:37.908 --> 00:08:39.828
And my story could matter
to a lot more people

00:08:39.828 --> 00:08:40.828
than I had imagined.

00:08:41.968 --> 00:08:43.508
- Yeah, I mean, that's an understatement

00:08:43.508 --> 00:08:44.498
and you know, it's funny.

00:08:44.498 --> 00:08:45.348
Cause even when you get, you know,

00:08:45.348 --> 00:08:46.678
200 books in a few months,

00:08:46.678 --> 00:08:47.668
it's not a, it's not

00:08:47.668 --> 00:08:50.790
a smaller achievement.
- It's not (indistinct)

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.008
I want people to know that
I was not happy with it

00:08:53.008 --> 00:08:54.988
because a thousand is not 200,

00:08:54.988 --> 00:08:57.998
but my parents said that
even if I only collected 200,

00:08:57.998 --> 00:08:59.208
we were still going to donate them.

00:08:59.208 --> 00:09:01.728
We were still going to
pursue the original goal

00:09:01.728 --> 00:09:03.288
because the goal is not necessarily,

00:09:03.288 --> 00:09:05.688
it is a thousand black
girl books in the title,

00:09:05.688 --> 00:09:07.668
but the goal is to learn how
to help change the world.

00:09:07.668 --> 00:09:09.748
The goal is to learn how
to give back to others

00:09:09.748 --> 00:09:10.798
and for a lot of kids,

00:09:10.798 --> 00:09:13.158
I think because I've gained
so much media attention,

00:09:13.158 --> 00:09:15.108
they feel as though they
have to set a thousand

00:09:15.108 --> 00:09:17.218
as their goal, or they
have to set a million.

00:09:17.218 --> 00:09:19.518
But in reality, it's
about taking your skills,

00:09:19.518 --> 00:09:20.768
taking the things you love,

00:09:20.768 --> 00:09:22.968
which for me was reading
and then applying them

00:09:22.968 --> 00:09:25.318
to problems you face and
frustrations you have.

00:09:26.808 --> 00:09:27.688
- No, absolutely.

00:09:27.688 --> 00:09:30.658
I can't overstate what you just said,

00:09:30.658 --> 00:09:32.408
you know, even the early
days of Khan Academy,

00:09:32.408 --> 00:09:34.818
I was happy when I was
able to help two or three

00:09:34.818 --> 00:09:37.618
of my one, one cousin and
then two or three cousins.

00:09:37.618 --> 00:09:38.608
But in the back of my mind says

00:09:38.608 --> 00:09:40.038
it's going to be useful for them.

00:09:40.038 --> 00:09:41.558
It could be useful for
a lot of other folks.

00:09:41.558 --> 00:09:43.578
And I think you're, you've gone on a very,

00:09:43.578 --> 00:09:45.078
a very, very similar journey.

00:09:45.078 --> 00:09:46.528
You know, there's a good question here

00:09:46.528 --> 00:09:49.898
from a YouTube Chloe
O'Neil and it, you know,

00:09:49.898 --> 00:09:53.473
you're clearly someone who
has a deep love of books,

00:09:53.473 --> 00:09:56.268
you know, how have books
affected your life?

00:09:56.268 --> 00:09:58.298
And I'll extend Chloe's question a bit,

00:09:58.298 --> 00:09:59.178
how have they affected your life?

00:09:59.178 --> 00:10:01.918
And you know, why do you
think this is so important,

00:10:01.918 --> 00:10:05.411
to see yourself or to see
diversity in these stories?

00:10:06.288 --> 00:10:08.038
- Well, I think reading is super important

00:10:08.038 --> 00:10:09.678
for so many reasons.

00:10:09.678 --> 00:10:11.568
I think one of the main things about

00:10:11.568 --> 00:10:13.128
why diversity is important is because

00:10:13.128 --> 00:10:15.158
so many kids do not like to read.

00:10:15.158 --> 00:10:16.228
They don't find it fun.

00:10:16.228 --> 00:10:18.568
And often reading is used as a punishment

00:10:18.568 --> 00:10:19.778
within the household.

00:10:19.778 --> 00:10:22.508
And we're often presented
with similar stories,

00:10:22.508 --> 00:10:24.368
hearing this, some of the
stories I was assigned,

00:10:24.368 --> 00:10:26.618
my teachers had read
when they were in school,

00:10:26.618 --> 00:10:27.848
which might seem fun,

00:10:27.848 --> 00:10:29.208
but we also need to think about

00:10:29.208 --> 00:10:30.691
how can we change and adapt to

00:10:30.691 --> 00:10:33.138
the ways that our classrooms now look,

00:10:33.138 --> 00:10:35.128
the ways that technology is involved.

00:10:35.128 --> 00:10:37.278
So reading is super important and having

00:10:37.278 --> 00:10:38.508
and supporting so many authors

00:10:38.508 --> 00:10:40.218
that are innovating in their writing,

00:10:40.218 --> 00:10:41.968
because you can connect to the experiences

00:10:41.968 --> 00:10:44.128
of so many different types of children.

00:10:44.128 --> 00:10:46.598
The books I was reading
mainly had white boys

00:10:46.598 --> 00:10:47.431
as the main character.

00:10:47.431 --> 00:10:49.238
And my quote, was kind
of that I only read books

00:10:49.238 --> 00:10:51.378
about white boys and their dogs.

00:10:51.378 --> 00:10:52.528
And although that might seem like

00:10:52.528 --> 00:10:54.468
it's just an independent experience

00:10:54.468 --> 00:10:57.338
over 80% of the books published in 2015,

00:10:57.338 --> 00:10:59.088
the year when I faced this problem,

00:10:59.088 --> 00:11:01.788
we're about animals and
we're about white people.

00:11:01.788 --> 00:11:04.928
So I realized now that when
we think about reading,

00:11:04.928 --> 00:11:06.978
we have to make sure that
we show all the aspects

00:11:06.978 --> 00:11:07.998
of different people.

00:11:07.998 --> 00:11:10.168
Native people's lives,
black people's lives,

00:11:10.168 --> 00:11:12.708
white people's lives,
women, girls, children,

00:11:12.708 --> 00:11:14.884
and then make sure that we
include in our classrooms.

00:11:14.884 --> 00:11:17.758
I'll look out into the
real world and to see

00:11:17.758 --> 00:11:20.988
our friends, our family,
strangers that we may admire,

00:11:20.988 --> 00:11:23.838
our heroes and that all these
people can look different

00:11:23.838 --> 00:11:25.648
and their experiences can be different.

00:11:25.648 --> 00:11:28.608
And difference does not mean a bad thing.

00:11:28.608 --> 00:11:30.968
It is actually a gift to
all of us to understand

00:11:30.968 --> 00:11:33.751
and see the perspectives
of new people and ideas.

00:11:35.088 --> 00:11:37.178
- And you know, now that
you've gone on this journey

00:11:37.178 --> 00:11:39.268
and you have these, you
know, many thousands of books

00:11:39.268 --> 00:11:40.288
that you've, you've been able to get.

00:11:40.288 --> 00:11:41.638
I mean, I'm assuming you're,

00:11:41.638 --> 00:11:43.258
you're reading a lot of these books.

00:11:43.258 --> 00:11:44.318
I have there been some stories.

00:11:44.318 --> 00:11:45.678
I mean, you mentioned
one is your favorite.

00:11:45.678 --> 00:11:46.708
I mean, what are some of the,

00:11:46.708 --> 00:11:48.938
I've encouraged everyone
to go to your website,

00:11:48.938 --> 00:11:50.398
thousands of black girl books,

00:11:50.398 --> 00:11:52.691
but what are some of
your favorites and why?

00:11:53.658 --> 00:11:55.439
- So some of my favorite personal books,

00:11:55.439 --> 00:11:56.605
one will always be

00:11:56.605 --> 00:11:58.528
"Brown Girl Dreaming"
by Jacqueline Woodson,

00:11:58.528 --> 00:12:00.858
which was kind of the exit and the purpose

00:12:00.858 --> 00:12:03.318
for starting the campaign,
because I loved it so much,

00:12:03.318 --> 00:12:05.078
but some of my favorite picture books

00:12:05.078 --> 00:12:07.065
I would say are probably

00:12:07.065 --> 00:12:10.343
"Please, Puppy, Please" by Spike Lee,

00:12:10.343 --> 00:12:13.505
I would say "President of the
Fifth Grade" by Sherri Winston

00:12:14.637 --> 00:12:16.868
"Piecing Me Together" by Renee Watson.

00:12:16.868 --> 00:12:18.678
I've read so many books now at this point.

00:12:18.678 --> 00:12:21.108
And I, I always read
the picture books first

00:12:21.108 --> 00:12:23.578
because you know, they're a
little bit easier for me to read

00:12:23.578 --> 00:12:25.208
now that I'm 15,

00:12:25.208 --> 00:12:27.268
but when you start to get
into those middle grade books,

00:12:27.268 --> 00:12:28.838
especially for middle schoolers,

00:12:28.838 --> 00:12:31.088
you realize how important that time is

00:12:31.088 --> 00:12:33.338
because your identity starts
to develop your point of view.

00:12:33.338 --> 00:12:35.228
You get more time to speak.

00:12:35.228 --> 00:12:37.538
You might have a more
eloquent and articulate

00:12:37.538 --> 00:12:38.838
and your ideas.

00:12:38.838 --> 00:12:40.238
And I always push for that,

00:12:40.238 --> 00:12:42.258
especially for kids that
are within, you know,

00:12:42.258 --> 00:12:43.898
fifth grade to eighth grade

00:12:43.898 --> 00:12:47.148
to start thinking about social
action as an everyday thing,

00:12:47.148 --> 00:12:49.558
because now you're starting
to recognize patterns

00:12:49.558 --> 00:12:50.618
and realize, Hey,

00:12:50.618 --> 00:12:52.898
people said the same thing
to me in elementary school,

00:12:52.898 --> 00:12:55.288
and I'm now facing the same
problem with middle school.

00:12:55.288 --> 00:12:57.168
How can I stop this from happening?

00:12:57.168 --> 00:12:59.098
So I have a resource guide.

00:12:59.098 --> 00:13:02.718
At a website called
grassrootscommunityfoundation.org

00:13:02.718 --> 00:13:04.448
But if you really are a little bit lazy,

00:13:04.448 --> 00:13:06.768
you can just look up
"1000 Black Girl Books"

00:13:06.768 --> 00:13:07.898
and it'll be there.

00:13:07.898 --> 00:13:10.588
But it has a list of over a thousand

00:13:10.588 --> 00:13:12.028
of the titles that we've collected

00:13:12.028 --> 00:13:14.428
that are separated by emerging reader,

00:13:14.428 --> 00:13:16.608
middle grade reader
and independent reader.

00:13:16.608 --> 00:13:18.118
So if you are in that middle grade,

00:13:18.118 --> 00:13:20.345
that fifth grade, eighth grade, three

00:13:20.345 --> 00:13:22.348
grade three through grade five,

00:13:22.348 --> 00:13:23.928
you can make sure that you find books

00:13:23.928 --> 00:13:25.428
that show these interesting perspectives

00:13:25.428 --> 00:13:26.868
and can help you find the words

00:13:26.868 --> 00:13:29.451
to describe some of the things
you're passionate about.

00:13:30.598 --> 00:13:32.208
- And what you know now that you've,

00:13:32.208 --> 00:13:33.998
I'm assuming these books
you're distributing

00:13:33.998 --> 00:13:36.048
in your local area to other places,

00:13:36.048 --> 00:13:39.868
have what you know have you
heard from students your age,

00:13:39.868 --> 00:13:40.701
or even younger

00:13:40.701 --> 00:13:41.795
that said, "Oh, thank you for doing this,

00:13:41.795 --> 00:13:42.848
"I never saw myself."

00:13:42.848 --> 00:13:45.198
What kind of feedback
have you been hearing?

00:13:45.198 --> 00:13:47.038
- It is, I am so happy

00:13:47.038 --> 00:13:49.138
that I'm still able to
hear feedback online,

00:13:49.138 --> 00:13:51.568
especially through Instagram
and DM and all these things.

00:13:51.568 --> 00:13:54.188
But when we got to actually
travel before COVID-19,

00:13:54.188 --> 00:13:56.488
it was so fun getting to meet kids,

00:13:56.488 --> 00:13:59.178
because I really, really
enjoyed that experience

00:13:59.178 --> 00:14:00.011
so much more.

00:14:00.011 --> 00:14:02.218
I think because people
don't have diversity,

00:14:02.218 --> 00:14:04.768
they don't necessarily
understand that it's an issue.

00:14:04.768 --> 00:14:06.328
So we get to go to schools in Chicago,

00:14:06.328 --> 00:14:08.198
or we get to go to
schools in Philadelphia.

00:14:08.198 --> 00:14:10.245
It is so much fun to see kids be like,

00:14:10.245 --> 00:14:12.055
"Oh my God, my hair looks just like that.

00:14:12.055 --> 00:14:13.775
"Oh, I wear glasses just like this.

00:14:13.775 --> 00:14:15.755
"Oh, I have these beautiful Brown eyes.

00:14:15.755 --> 00:14:17.495
"And this cover of the book has a girl

00:14:17.495 --> 00:14:18.478
"with beautiful Brown eyes."

00:14:18.478 --> 00:14:21.148
So it's really amazing to see

00:14:21.148 --> 00:14:22.618
black kids get to see themselves,

00:14:22.618 --> 00:14:24.338
but it's also really cool

00:14:24.338 --> 00:14:26.978
to get to see kids of different
races and experiences.

00:14:26.978 --> 00:14:29.448
Talk about why my, how
much they love those books

00:14:29.448 --> 00:14:30.848
and how much they enjoy them.

00:14:30.848 --> 00:14:33.198
Because obviously race
is not the only thing

00:14:33.198 --> 00:14:34.878
that is super important
about these stories,

00:14:34.878 --> 00:14:36.358
but it matters in our everyday life,

00:14:36.358 --> 00:14:37.498
and our differences are something

00:14:37.498 --> 00:14:39.268
that's worth being celebrated.

00:14:39.268 --> 00:14:42.268
So it's really awesome to see
kids that are not black girls

00:14:42.268 --> 00:14:44.888
really understanding, empathizing with

00:14:44.888 --> 00:14:48.011
and caring about the experiences
of girls that look like me.

00:14:49.528 --> 00:14:51.898
- Oh, that's, that's
super, super powerful.

00:14:51.898 --> 00:14:54.558
You know, not only are you an
avid reader and a collector,

00:14:54.558 --> 00:14:56.528
I guess you could say a curator of books.

00:14:56.528 --> 00:14:57.361
But now you...
- Yeah,

00:14:57.361 --> 00:14:58.485
I could say collector
almost at this point,

00:14:58.485 --> 00:14:59.478
you can say collector.

00:14:59.478 --> 00:15:02.308
I have so many books now,
personal home as well

00:15:02.308 --> 00:15:04.158
that I think you could say collector.

00:15:05.218 --> 00:15:06.438
- I'm imagining these stacks

00:15:06.438 --> 00:15:07.868
of thousands of books all around you,

00:15:07.868 --> 00:15:10.728
which, your shelf behind
you is more organized,

00:15:10.728 --> 00:15:13.099
but I, you got, I'm assuming
these thousands of books are.

00:15:13.099 --> 00:15:14.008
(indistinct)

00:15:14.008 --> 00:15:16.168
- I have like a whole thing full of books

00:15:16.168 --> 00:15:18.328
because whenever I get
a signed copy from like,

00:15:18.328 --> 00:15:21.228
especially self-published
authors or independent authors,

00:15:21.228 --> 00:15:23.928
I keep all of them in my
home, in my personal library,

00:15:23.928 --> 00:15:25.218
because I don't want these people to think

00:15:25.218 --> 00:15:26.538
that they don't matter to me.

00:15:26.538 --> 00:15:28.248
And then all the other books get donated

00:15:28.248 --> 00:15:29.368
that we have their copies for.

00:15:29.368 --> 00:15:32.868
So I keep every, at least
one copy of almost every book

00:15:32.868 --> 00:15:35.901
I try to keep that I really
like and, and interested in.

00:15:37.498 --> 00:15:41.248
- No, I'd love to see that
stack sometime, you know,

00:15:41.248 --> 00:15:43.678
but you're not only a
curator collector of books,

00:15:43.678 --> 00:15:47.018
but now you're going off and
you're writing now, you know,

00:15:47.018 --> 00:15:51.598
there's another question
from YouTube Taupin Airlines.

00:15:51.598 --> 00:15:53.868
I don't know if they're in
airlines or not, but he said,

00:15:53.868 --> 00:15:56.921
what made you wanna write a
book or write, be a writer?

00:15:57.848 --> 00:16:00.168
- So kind of the reason
why I wanted to write

00:16:00.168 --> 00:16:01.328
Marley Dias gets done.

00:16:01.328 --> 00:16:04.108
And so can you, was because
all the time I get asked,

00:16:04.108 --> 00:16:04.948
how do you do it?

00:16:04.948 --> 00:16:05.781
How do you do it?

00:16:05.781 --> 00:16:06.768
What are the steps?

00:16:06.768 --> 00:16:09.138
And it might be nice to say
it to one person, you know,

00:16:09.138 --> 00:16:11.348
after I do an event or I talked to them

00:16:11.348 --> 00:16:13.248
and I get to have that
independent interaction,

00:16:13.248 --> 00:16:15.668
but I wanted to put that
information out there.

00:16:15.668 --> 00:16:17.128
And I wrote the book when I was 12.

00:16:17.128 --> 00:16:19.758
So it's not necessarily
the most updated version

00:16:19.758 --> 00:16:21.498
of my methodologies or beliefs,

00:16:21.498 --> 00:16:25.028
but it really captures the
passion and the interest I have

00:16:25.028 --> 00:16:26.748
in social action and reading.

00:16:26.748 --> 00:16:28.548
And it talks a lot about my family,

00:16:28.548 --> 00:16:30.248
which is something that
is super important to me,

00:16:30.248 --> 00:16:33.198
for people to understand
is really a part of

00:16:33.198 --> 00:16:34.628
my guiding principles in life.

00:16:34.628 --> 00:16:37.998
What I believe in, what I care
about and how important it is

00:16:37.998 --> 00:16:40.108
that we have caregivers and educators

00:16:40.108 --> 00:16:42.218
that invest in children's curiosity,

00:16:42.218 --> 00:16:44.208
their imagination and their leadership.

00:16:44.208 --> 00:16:46.528
And because I've had
all those experiences,

00:16:46.528 --> 00:16:47.868
I really wanted to put it out there

00:16:47.868 --> 00:16:50.618
and emphasize the importance
of sharing that with kids

00:16:50.618 --> 00:16:52.458
and for kids that might
feel like it's too late.

00:16:52.458 --> 00:16:53.678
My parents didn't help me with this.

00:16:53.678 --> 00:16:56.528
And I don't feel like I can,
providing them with solutions

00:16:56.528 --> 00:16:59.187
and options to work with
their friends, their families,

00:16:59.187 --> 00:17:01.018
and other members of their community

00:17:01.018 --> 00:17:02.718
to create social change,

00:17:02.718 --> 00:17:05.578
to feel confident in their
ideas and to hopefully

00:17:05.578 --> 00:17:07.241
make some meaningful impacts.

00:17:08.148 --> 00:17:09.858
- So Marley Dias gets it done.

00:17:09.858 --> 00:17:11.678
I mean, that actually sounds
like a political slogan.

00:17:11.678 --> 00:17:13.969
I'll ask you about that a
little bit, but the, the,

00:17:13.969 --> 00:17:15.611
but is that what it is?

00:17:15.611 --> 00:17:16.748
It's kind of like a handbook of like,

00:17:16.748 --> 00:17:17.998
Hey, if you're a young person

00:17:17.998 --> 00:17:19.068
or actually not on a young person,

00:17:19.068 --> 00:17:22.583
you're just a person who wants
to do stuff in the world.

00:17:22.583 --> 00:17:25.371
You know, this is, this is one
way of tackling, it is that,

00:17:25.371 --> 00:17:26.648
is that the goal of the book

00:17:26.648 --> 00:17:27.770
to get more people to take action.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.378
- Yes, the goal of the
book is to be a guide book,

00:17:29.378 --> 00:17:31.468
but it's also really important to me.

00:17:31.468 --> 00:17:33.858
And I, I think it's kind of
an underrated part of the book

00:17:33.858 --> 00:17:36.438
is to celebrate the gratitude I have

00:17:36.438 --> 00:17:38.288
for other activists
that have come before me

00:17:38.288 --> 00:17:40.518
talking about women like Augusta Baker,

00:17:40.518 --> 00:17:41.351
who was one of the,

00:17:41.351 --> 00:17:43.598
she used to be a librarian for
the New York Public Library.

00:17:43.598 --> 00:17:46.278
And she was so focused on
diversity and inclusion

00:17:46.278 --> 00:17:47.558
post the Harlem Renaissance

00:17:47.558 --> 00:17:50.328
and talking about Bob
Marley, who I'm named after

00:17:50.328 --> 00:17:52.578
and Jamaican revolution, like, you know,

00:17:52.578 --> 00:17:54.161
there's, there's so many
little tidbits I have

00:17:54.161 --> 00:17:57.248
and the book is full of
color, it's full of pictures

00:17:57.248 --> 00:17:58.538
and it's inclusive to people that

00:17:58.538 --> 00:18:00.008
don't necessarily like to read.

00:18:00.008 --> 00:18:01.708
And aren't gonna pick up a book,

00:18:01.708 --> 00:18:04.448
that's this heavy with no pictures.

00:18:04.448 --> 00:18:07.798
So I made sure to make it a
guide book for kids, obviously,

00:18:07.798 --> 00:18:09.538
but then for anybody, like you said,

00:18:09.538 --> 00:18:11.488
that's interested in changing the world

00:18:11.488 --> 00:18:13.038
or wants to know more about me.

00:18:14.638 --> 00:18:16.988
- It's very cool that you
were named after Bob Marley.

00:18:16.988 --> 00:18:19.758
- I don't think my mum (indistinct)

00:18:19.758 --> 00:18:21.448
she named me after Bob Marley, so I can,

00:18:21.448 --> 00:18:23.338
I will never forget my Jamaican heritage

00:18:23.338 --> 00:18:25.331
and the pride I have in being Jamaican.

00:18:26.298 --> 00:18:30.518
- Absolutely. You know, a
question here from YouTube

00:18:30.518 --> 00:18:32.908
and, you know, anyone who's
does something in the world,

00:18:32.908 --> 00:18:34.488
especially something that's public

00:18:34.488 --> 00:18:35.718
you're bound to get, you know,

00:18:35.718 --> 00:18:37.258
have some setbacks and criticisms.

00:18:37.258 --> 00:18:39.898
So this question from
YouTube, Yeadon Youth says,

00:18:39.898 --> 00:18:42.398
did you experience any
negative feedback from people?

00:18:42.398 --> 00:18:44.401
If so, how have you handled it?

00:18:45.288 --> 00:18:47.278
- So I do experience
some negative feedback.

00:18:47.278 --> 00:18:50.018
I think one of the, the things
I hear the most is like,

00:18:50.018 --> 00:18:51.968
why does it have to be about race?

00:18:51.968 --> 00:18:54.028
Why does it, like, why does it matter?

00:18:54.028 --> 00:18:56.388
And I, I get that
question, I understand it.

00:18:56.388 --> 00:18:57.635
But I think whenever people ask that

00:18:57.635 --> 00:19:00.668
and they're insinuating that
something about race is bad

00:19:00.668 --> 00:19:02.048
or talking about race,

00:19:02.048 --> 00:19:03.548
but it's not that at all.

00:19:03.548 --> 00:19:04.908
And I, I think the more that we make

00:19:04.908 --> 00:19:06.618
these conversations natural,

00:19:06.618 --> 00:19:08.518
and don't treat talking about and saying

00:19:08.518 --> 00:19:10.168
that I'm proud to be black or saying

00:19:10.168 --> 00:19:12.998
that I'm proud to be a white
or from any other country

00:19:12.998 --> 00:19:14.568
or any other region.

00:19:14.568 --> 00:19:15.498
It is a problem.

00:19:15.498 --> 00:19:18.488
So what I try to do in this
conversation to not say

00:19:18.488 --> 00:19:20.938
that I'm a girl of color
or a woman of color,

00:19:20.938 --> 00:19:22.818
but I'm proud of being a black girl.

00:19:22.818 --> 00:19:24.688
And that's the way that
I'm perceived in the world

00:19:24.688 --> 00:19:26.718
in showing pride in who I am

00:19:26.718 --> 00:19:28.128
is something that's
really important to me.

00:19:28.128 --> 00:19:29.998
So it can bother me sometimes.

00:19:29.998 --> 00:19:30.831
People say that,

00:19:30.831 --> 00:19:33.728
because they insinuate that
talking about race is bad.

00:19:33.728 --> 00:19:35.718
